Brute Force
-----------
Idea:
- Split the string into words.
- Store each word in a vector.
- Return the length of the last word.

Algorithm:
1. Traverse the string.
2. Build words character by character.
3. Whenever a space is encountered, store the current word.
4. Return the length of the last stored word.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLastWord(string s) {
        vector<string> words;
        string word = "";

        for (char ch : s) {
            if (ch == ' ') {
                if (!word.empty()) {
                    words.push_back(word);
                    word = "";
                }
            } else {
                word += ch;
            }
        }

        if (!word.empty())
            words.push_back(word);

        return words.back().size();
    }
};
```

Better Solution
---------------
Idea:
- Traverse the string from the end.
- Ignore trailing spaces.
- Count characters until the next space.

Algorithm:
1. Start from the last index.
2. Skip trailing spaces.
3. Count characters until a space or beginning of string.
4. Return the count.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLastWord(string s) {
        int i = s.size() - 1;

        while (i >= 0 && s[i] == ' ')
            i--;

        int length = 0;

        while (i >= 0 && s[i] != ' ') {
            length++;
            i--;
        }

        return length;
    }
};
```

Optimal Solution
----------------
No intermediate improvement exists. The reverse traversal approach is already the optimal solution.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Interview Notes
---------------
- The brute-force approach stores all words unnecessarily.
- Traversing from the end avoids extra space and immediately finds the last word.
- Always skip trailing spaces first, as the input may end with one or more spaces.
- Edge Cases:
  - Single word.
  - Multiple spaces between words.
  - Trailing spaces.
  - One-character word.
- Follow-up:
  - Can you solve it without using `split()` or extra space?
    - Yes, by traversing the string from the end.
- This is a simple string traversal problem commonly asked to test attention to edge cases.
